<!--
	The mode switch of the agent security test bed: turns tool authorization OFF while keeping the
	whole fixture in place.

	Add it to "WEB-INF/conf/metaConf.txt" on the line AFTER "secdemo.config.xml":

		secdemo.config.xml
		secdemo-legacy.config.xml

	and remove that one line again to go back to enforcement. Nothing else changes - the personas,
	the roles, the model rights, the tool policy and the seeded data are all still there, so the
	same login and the same prompt can be compared side by side under both modes. That is the point:
	the difference you observe is then attributable to the mode and to nothing else.

	How it works, in one property. AiToolAccessService resolves its ceiling from the policy: an
	explicitly configured "security-mode" always wins, and only in its absence does a non-empty
	<tool-access> mean ENFORCED. Setting the property to LEGACY here therefore overrides the
	resolution without touching the matrix - the boot log still reports "roles=6", and it reports
	"mode=LEGACY ... (not enforced)".

	What LEGACY means, precisely:
	  * every tool an agent carries is callable by every account that can use that agent,
	  * the per-turn schema filter withholds nothing,
	  * no skill is dropped and loadSkill hands over every tool it resolves,
	  * the "### Capability Limits" section of the system prompt is not emitted at all, so the
	    prompt is byte-identical to what it was before the tool layer existed,
	  * the MCP endpoint executes tools again,
	  * the engine's model access rights are UNAFFECTED. They are not tl-ai's layer and this file
	    does not reach them: secreader1 still cannot create a note and still sees no secret. Only
	    the *tool* layer goes quiet.

	See doc/security/testbed.md, section "Switching between LEGACY and ENFORCED".
-->
<application xmlns:config="http://www.top-logic.com/ns/config/6.0">
	<services>
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.security.AiToolAccessService">
			<instance security-mode="LEGACY"/>
		</config>
	</services>
</application>
